<h2>The incident</h2>
<p>On the night of 15 October 2021, a firing incident at Mahavir Enclave, Dwarka, left one man — Afroz Alam — dead on arrival at hospital. The complainant&rsquo;s account was that a quarrel over motorcycle parking escalated: the applicant allegedly assaulted him with a wooden plank, while co-accused Bilal @ Raja fired the fatal shot. The applicant was arrested on 2 November 2021 and chargesheeted in January 2022 under Sections 302, 307 and 34 IPC with Arms Act offences.</p>

<h2>Why regular bail failed</h2>
<p>The defence attacked the credibility of the witnesses and the attribution of the firing. The court, however, held that the applicant&rsquo;s role was &ldquo;not peripheral&rdquo; and disclosed active participation with common intention under Section 34 IPC (para 7). Three further factors sealed the outcome: the nominal roll recorded four other pending FIRs; a conviction stood against him in a 2019 case; and seven incidents of jail misconduct were on record (paras 14-16). The court relied on <em>Neeru Yadav v. State of U.P.</em>, (2016) 15 SCC 422, where the Supreme Court set aside bail granted to a history-sheeter, holding that criminal antecedents are an independent and weighty consideration (para 17). A co-accused&rsquo;s earlier bail was distinguished on role.</p>

<h2>Why interim bail succeeded</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>The applicant has a metal implant in his left knee, and the Senior Medical Officer of Central Jail No. 1, in a report dated 30 September 2026, supported the need for treatment. The court granted interim bail for three weeks on a personal bond of Rs 25,000 with two sureties, with conditions and a direction to surrender before the jail authorities by 4:00 pm on the expiry of the period (paras 20-22).</p>
</div>
<p>The two outcomes are not in tension. Regular bail asks whether the accused should be at liberty pending trial — a question answered by role, evidence and antecedents. Interim medical bail asks a narrower question: does a documented medical condition require treatment that custody cannot adequately provide, for a defined period? An accused who fails the first test may still satisfy the second, and vice versa.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Medical interim bail stands or falls on the jail medical record — the SMO&rsquo;s report, treatment history and the specificity of the proposed procedure. Vague pleas of ill health rarely move the court.</li>
  <li>The nominal roll is read closely in Delhi: pending FIRs, prior convictions and jail conduct entries should be addressed head-on, not left for the prosecution to spring.</li>
  <li>Parity arguments must engage with role. Courts routinely distinguish co-accused bail orders where the applicant&rsquo;s attributed acts differ.</li>
  <li>Timed surrender conditions are strictly enforced; an overstay invites cancellation proceedings and colours every future application.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The larger picture</h2>
<p>Delhi&rsquo;s sessions dockets carry many part-heard murder trials in which bail applications are renewed as evidence progresses. This order illustrates the court&rsquo;s working method: assess role on the chargesheet as it stands, weigh antecedents through the nominal roll, and keep the humanitarian valve of interim medical bail available — bounded in time, secured by sureties, and detached from the merits. Accused persons and their families should understand that a medical release is a bridge for treatment, not a judicial signal about the strength of the case.</p>
